>>
endobj
674 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (Every one of us is here today thanks to someone who menstruates, yet menstruation is still a taboo subject among young people.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
675 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (It is up to all of us to finally change that.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
676 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (Menstruation is a natural and completely normal occurrence.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
677 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (The shame and discomfort surrounding it are learned, but that doesn't mean we actually need to feel them. When we experience these feelings, it becomes harder to talk about pain and other symptoms, ask for help when we need it, or speak up when we don't have access to menstrual products.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
678 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (This can also lead to the use of improvised or inadequate alternatives, which carry serious health risks.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
679 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (We call this problem period poverty.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
680 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (Period poverty is not just a lack of access to menstrual products, but also to adequate hygiene, education, or sanitary facilities due to financial or other barriers.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
681 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (When we talk about menstruation more openly, we create an environment where it is easier to ask for help, support, advice, or a menstrual product when we need one.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
682 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 182 0 R
/Pg 38 0 R
/A [1318 0 R 0]
/K [8]
>>
endobj
683 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (Glossary of Terms)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
684 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (What is a speculum?)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
685 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (A speculum is a medical instrument \(made of metal or plastic\) used to gently open and separate the walls of a body cavity, such as the vagina, allowing the gynecologist to examine the inside of the vagina and the cervix, as well as collect swab samples.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
686 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (Although a speculum examination may feel somewhat uncomfortable, it should not be painful.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
687 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (There are different sizes of speculums available, and you have the right to ask for a different size if the one being used is not comfortable for you.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
688 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 183 0 R
/Pg 39 0 R
/A [1319 0 R 0]
/K [5]
>>
endobj
689 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 184 0 R
/E (What is a Pap smear/test?)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
690 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 184 0 R
/E (A Pap \(Papanicolaou\) test is a simple procedure in which a small sample of cells is collected from the cervix to detect early changes that may require monitoring or treatment.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
691 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 184 0 R
/Pg 40 0 R
/A [1320 0 R 0]
/K [2]
>>
endobj
692 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 184 0 R
/E (What are HPV infection and the HPV vaccine?)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
693 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 184 0 R
/E (HPV \(Human Papillomavirus\) is a group of viruses that are transmitted through sexual contact. They can infect the skin and mucous membranes and may lead to the development of abnormal cells and cancers of the cervix, vulva, vagina, penis, anus, mouth, or throat.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
694 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 184 0 R
/E (Importantly, many HPV infections and related diseases can be prevented through vaccination. If you would like to learn more about this important topic, you can visit hpvinfo.rs and/or talk to a gynecologist about what is best for you.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
695 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 184 0 R
/Pg 40 0 R
/A [1321 0 R 0]
/K [6]
>>
endobj
696 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E (There are different types of contraception:)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
697 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1322 0 R
/E (Barrier methods \(condoms\), the only method that also protects against sexually transmitted infections.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
698 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1323 0 R
/E (Hormonal methods \(contraceptive pills, patches, vaginal ring, injections\), which act on the hormonal cycle and prevent ovulation.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
699 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1324 0 R
/E (Intrauterine devices \(IUDs\), hormonal and non-hormonal, which prevent fertilization and/or implantation.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
700 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1325 0 R
/E (Emergency contraception, used after unprotected intercourse within a limited time frame.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
701 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
702 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E (What is contraception?)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
703 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E (Contraception is a set of methods and devices used to prevent conception, or an unintended pregnancy. It works by preventing ovulation, fertilization, or the implantation of a fertilized egg in the uterus.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
